\begin_layout Standard
In the self-employment extension we introduce an education-specific entrepreneur closure risk,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{e}(h)$
\end_inset

.
 Direct U.S.
 estimates by education are limited,
 so the calibration is constructed in two transparent steps.
 First,
 official U.S.
 business survival evidence from the BLS and Census implies substantial startup exit,
 so we set a conservative average annual entrepreneur closure rate of 0.15,
 below first-year startup exit because the model includes incumbent businesses as well as new entrants.
 Second,
 we use the competing-risk estimates in Grashuis (2021) for transitions from self-employment to unemployment to impose a modest education gradient.
 Mapping the model's three education groups into high school or less,
 some college,
 and bachelor's or more,
 and normalizing the relative hazards to the model's education shares,
 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{e}(h)=\{0.168,0.150,0.144\}$
\end_inset

.
 In the benchmark interpretation,
 entrepreneur closure moves the household out of business but does not automatically confer regular unemployment insurance eligibility,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This is intended to match regular U.S.
 unemployment insurance rules rather than special temporary programs.
 Regular UI eligibility is generally tied to prior covered wage employment,
 whereas purely self-employed earnings are not automatically covered in the same way.
 The institutional benchmark comes from U.S.
 Department of Labor eligibility guidance and IRS treatment of unemployment taxes.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 so the closure shock disciplines entrepreneurial survival without mechanically expanding UI receipt.We treat this as an initial calibration rule rather than a directly estimatedparameter,
 and use a modest gradient because the broader literature on educationand business survival is mixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The self-employment branch also allows for an employer-threshold cost,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{hire}$
\end_inset

,
 paid whenever the entrepreneur hires strictly positive labor.
 This is separate from the smooth search-and-matching hiring cost governed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 captures proportional vacancy and replacement costs,
 whereas 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{hire}$
\end_inset

 captures the discrete hurdle of becoming an employer rather than remaining self-employed.
 The literature supports this distinction,
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Recent evidence on the first-worker margin shows that labor costs can strongly affect the decision to become an employer.
 Cockx and Desiere (2024) exploit a Belgian reform that reduced the labor cost of the first employee by 13 percent and find a 31 percent jump in first-time employer entry,
 implying an elasticity of about -2.39.
 In related U.S.
 evidence,
 Guo and Wallskog (2025) show that even new-employer payroll taxes averaging about $350 per worker reduce first-worker hiring,
 while Harju,
 Matikka,
 and Rauhanen (2019) show that entrepreneurs respond strongly to compliance costs around tax thresholds.
 These papers motivate an employer-threshold cost,
 but do not pin down a structural dollar-equivalent fixed cost for our model.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 but direct structural calibration of a fixed employer cost remains limited.
 We therefore treat 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{hire}$
\end_inset

 as a disciplined experimental choice rather than a directly estimated parameter.
 In the current benchmark with closure risk and partial capital loss we set 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{hire}=0$
\end_inset

,
 while the first sensitivity run sets 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{hire}=0.25$
\end_inset

 in model units to test whether a modest employer hurdle improves the self-employed versus employer composition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Baseline Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 The baseline equilibrium shows moderate entrepreneurship (0.1781 overall),
 and the benchmark self-employment calibration places that margin inside entrepreneurship itself.
 In the benchmark,
 57.7 percent of entrepreneurs are self-employed nonemployers and 42.3 percent are employer entrepreneurs.
 Throughout the paper,
 entrepreneur is the broad category,
 while self-employed refers to the nonemployer subset of entrepreneurs.
 The composition differs sharply by education:
 low-education entrepreneurs are mostly self-employed,
 middle-education entrepreneurs are split more evenly,
 and high-education entrepreneurs are predominantly employer entrepreneurs.
 Average entrepreneur labor demand is 6.1587 workers,
 so the key object in this branch is not a large aggregate entrepreneurship boom but the composition of entrepreneurial activity across business types and education groups.
 The self-employment version of the model adds a second composition margin within entrepreneurship itself:
 whether entrants remain nonemployer self-employed or cross the threshold into employer entrepreneurship.
 That margin is central for interpreting the closure-risk and employer-threshold exercises that follow,
 even when changes in aggregate entry are modest.
\end_layout

